{
  "statement": "Silicon Valley panic over artificial intelligence is not a scientific breakthrough. It is an insular religious panic inherited directly from Eliezer Yudkowsky and the Rationalist subculture. When tech executives warn about superintelligent systems wiping out humanity, they are recycling theological eschatology from 2006 LessWrong forum posts, not reporting empirical lab results. That apocalyptic theater lets boardrooms pose as reluctant stewards while dodging accountability for shipping buggy software. And it gets worse because that exact moral panic provides the intellectual scaffolding for regulatory moats. Legacy giants embrace safety compliance regimes precisely because million-dollar audit committees lock out open-source competitors overnight. That is regulatory capture dressed up as civilizational caution.\n\nEvery month regulators delay open-weight model deployment, people die of untreated diseases that automated drug discovery could cure. Stanford researchers showed AlphaFold solved protein folding for 200 million known proteins in 2022, cutting decades of biological research down to minutes. Yet doomsday alarmists demand 18-month moratoriums on frontier compute clusters to study hypothetical sci-fi risks instead of deploying these life-saving models into clinical trials. That choice has a body count. When policymakers freeze open development out of abstract fear, they actively condemn cancer patients and diabetics to preventable suffering. Who gave self-appointed risk theorists the moral authority to trade real human lives for zero-risk whiteboard fantasies?\n\nDecentralized open-source development beats centralized corporate gatekeeping every single time. Meta shipped Llama 3 to millions of developers globally, sparking local deployments that proprietary API labs like OpenAI cannot censor or monopolize. Small research labs in Berlin and Bangalore now run frontier-class inference locally without paying a tech monopoly rent. We do not need state-licensed cartels dictating who gets to build software. We need open compute clusters, permissionless innovation, and an immediate end to safetyist roadblocks.",
