=== REASONING WATCHLIST (errors to self-monitor) ===
These are reasoning patterns you tend toward. Self-monitor — if you catch yourself using one, flag it explicitly rather than letting it pass.
- [skp-beliefs-167] Alarmist, Optimistic, and Political Framings of AI Serve Distinct Agendas: Genetic fallacy — The node implies that identifying the agenda behind a narrative is sufficient to undermine its validity — but the origin or motivation of a claim does not determine its truth. Alarmist rhetoric about AI risks could still be correct even if it serves particular interests.
- [skp-beliefs-057] Public Resistance to AI Reflects Accumulated Distrust from Prior Tech Cycles: Post hoc ergo propter hoc — The claim assumes that because prior tech cycles preceded current AI distrust, those cycles caused it—but contemporary AI skepticism may stem primarily from AI-specific concerns (job displacement, deepfakes, LLM hallucinations) rather than inherited grievances.
- [skp-beliefs-143] AI Skepticism Reflects Rational Risk Calibration, Not Technophobia: No True Scotsman / Definitional Gerrymandering — By defining AI skepticism as inherently 'rational risk calibration,' the claim insulates skeptical positions from criticism — any skeptic who turns out to be irrationally fearful can simply be excluded from the category, making the claim unfalsifiable.
- [skp-beliefs-125] Machine-Generated Fear About Machines Distorts Public AI Discourse: Correlation/Causation — The node infers a causal feedback loop from the co-occurrence of AI systems generating content and elevated public fear, without ruling out the simpler explanation that fear is driven by human journalists, researchers, and commentators responding to real events.
- [skp-beliefs-129] Safety Rhetoric as Instrument for Regulatory Consolidation: Genetic fallacy — The argument risks dismissing existential risk concerns entirely on the basis of who funds or promotes them, rather than engaging with the arguments on their merits. Even if some proponents benefit commercially, that does not establish the falsity of their safety claims.
- [skp-beliefs-113] Institutional Inertia From Entrenched Lobbies Prevents Regulatory Frameworks From Evolving: Survivorship bias — The claim foregrounds visible cases where entrenched lobbies blocked beneficial regulatory adaptation while systematically ignoring counterfactuals where stable regulatory frameworks prevented harmful market failures, crises, or negative externalities. The absence of disasters attributable to regulatory stability is structurally invisible.
- [skp-beliefs-156] AI Providers Strategically Locate in Low-Oversight Jurisdictions to Minimize Compliance Costs: Hasty Generalization — The claim extrapolates from well-documented patterns in corporate tax and financial-sector arbitrage to AI providers without demonstrating that AI companies have actually relocated operations primarily to evade AI-specific regulation at scale.
- [skp-desires-078] Foundational AI as a Public Good: Nirvana Fallacy — The desire implicitly contrasts an idealized public-good model against the failures of market concentration, without rigorously accounting for the well-documented failures of centralized or federated public infrastructure at scale.
- [skp-desires-005] Distributed AI Power and Democratic Infrastructure: Nirvana fallacy — The node contrasts the flawed reality of concentrated corporate AI power against an idealized vision of democratic oversight and distributed control, without adequately accounting for the practical failures, inefficiencies, or corruption risks of the proposed alternative governance structures.
- [skp-desires-069] Commons-Based Economic Alternatives to AI Capitalism: Nirvana fallacy — The proposal contrasts the flawed existing capitalist system against an idealized post-capitalist commons without adequately accounting for the practical governance failures, coordination costs, and capture risks that alternative systems also face.

